{
  "name": "Schrödinger soliton phase collider",
  "description": "Focusing saturable Schrödinger: four straight channels (V = −0.6, the ring track's depth), each with two identical solitons meeting head-on slowly. The only difference between the lanes is the right-hand soliton's phase relative to the left one: 0° (top), 90°, 180°, 270° (bottom). Measured in the lab: in phase (0°) their fields add, they merge into one bright peak and then come out again, equal in power (in open space at this speed, the nls-collisions scene, they stay fused). At 180° the fields cancel in the middle: a dark node keeps them about 19 cells apart, and they bounce back with equal power. At 90° they trade power, ending about 890 against 520 (out of 2 × 700), and the weaker one is flung out fast; 270° is the mirror image, the other one winning. Faster solitons pass through whatever the phase. The border absorbs. Try: Display → mode → reagent + param outlines to see the channels; the soliton stamps (four phases; still, slow in 8 headings, fast in 4) to stage your own collisions.",
